T={
'G01':"Anything Frank says that states a fact about someone becomes a told version in the hearer's memory, with Frank as its source, believed by fit and by his regard, and passed on by the gossip rules (2.4.1) like any telling. Telling someone to pass it on, paying a talker, staging a scene for watchers, or feeding one man a story to see where it comes out all follow from this. His conduct (giving a man up, crossing the road to avoid someone, a good deed) is a deed the street tells too. Found false, a claim lowers his regard with whoever learns it and can become a hook on him.",
'G02':"What Frank overhears (a bench, a bus stop, the caff, the barber, the dole queue) is drawn from facts the speakers really hold, at the strength they hold them, never from a canned pool. Money seen, a lost load, a fire, a fight: any sighting becomes a slot the seer holds and tells. Talk Frank watches or hears for a while goes into the Ledger as notes. A told version can carry wrong details in poor conditions; gossip never grows above what was seen (his ruling).",
'G03':"Every claim, to anyone, is weighed against what the hearer already holds: fit with known facts, the speaker's regard and menace, the hearer's nerve. This one rule runs police interviews (alibis checked against witnesses, silence, a crewman confessing to Frank's crime), lies to friends, two versions of a story compared in the launderette, bluffs, blame shifted, false impressions staged. A lie found out is a held fact against the liar.",
'G04':"Favours, debts, payments, bribes and promises are one ledger of kept obligations between named people. Anyone can be asked for a small act for money, a favour or pressure, decided by the same fold rule as a squeeze; repeated asks wear thin; a bought man asks again by greed; protection is an agreement with terms both sides hold; Frank can lend, pay off someone's debt, keep a tab or a shop's tick book, and break his word at a cost the town hears of.",
'G05':"A hook is a held fact the person would not want told, from what Frank saw, overheard or found (a letter, a photograph, a page in a dispatch book). It bites by what the person stands to lose against his nerve and grudge, is spent once used, can be sold back, and a man with nothing left to lose cannot be hooked. Affairs stay talk, never shown (content rule).",
'G06':"Some women, by their dials and their regard for Frank, can be courted; women only, not heavily scripted (his words as relayed in the advisor's brief of 25 Nov; not yet in RULINGS.md); no sexual content (canon). A courting state moves on calls at hours her routine allows, on what is said and on what she sees and hears of him through the town's ordinary talk. She can refuse, cool, end it or end it kindly; what she learns of his business reaches her by gossip and is weighed by her law dial.",
'G07':"Regard moves on deeds, read through each person's temper: small kindnesses (a cigarette, a tea, a lift, coal for the office), mercy, keeping habits (paying on Friday), amends, paying for a widow's flowers. The same ladder (notice, watch, comment, avoid, refuse, confront) runs for what anyone holds about anyone, so a believed rumour that a foreman grasses makes his crew avoid him; threats are weighed by nerve everywhere; amends move regard, never erase memory.",
'G08':"Ties between people are data: kin, friendship, crew pairings, grudges, rival factions' relations to each other. Ties decide who gathers (a wake, a funeral, a month's mind, a street collection), who steps in or chases a snatcher, who avenges, and what happens when two people Frank wronged meet. Factions hold ladders toward each other, so a truce can be brokered and broken and a leak can set two gangs on each other.",
'G09':"Every named person holds a short past of their own (a fact or two, a habit, a weekly spot) that the claim check reads, so an old woman can tell Frank a Mickey the crew never mentions, within the ruled facts of his death. Content written into those sheets, not a new system.",
'G10':"Rivals, crew, informants and robbers use Frank's verbs by Frank's rules: rivals plant men, buy informants, read his absence as an opening and act on what their own people hold; each crewman on a job keeps his own temper (holds, freezes, skims, goes too far); orders reach crew only through what they hear; hiring, docking pay and sacking are talk acts with a stated breaking point.",
'G11':"Dress is a claim to a role, read against place: a donkey jacket passes at the dock gate, a good suit is served first, any uniform that can be got (decision 13) is taken at face value by strangers and checked by those who should know the wearer. Every mark (coat, cap, haircut, voice, height, walk, a car's plate, colour and dent) is a separate slot matched two at a time; a change of appearance sheds slots only out of sight; a lost cap points to whoever is known for it.",
'G12':"One sight and sound map serves every watcher. It covers light (lamps, a power cut, the light states by hour), his three weathers, windows and net curtains, walls, crowds and noise as cover, footwear on each surface, and alarms and lures by reach. Each witness's account is built from his own slots, with errors in poor conditions and borrowed details after talking to another witness. Watchers learn patterns from what they remember.",
'G13':"Objects carry their owner and the facts of how they moved: a stolen wallet, a planted parcel, a coat, a letter, a body, a dropped glove. Owners know their things anywhere; anyone may find what is left in sight; evidence keeps a chain the police can follow; lifting and planting small things is one verb with readable limits, if he says yes (decision 12); forgeries come only through a person who remembers making them. Each trace kind has a life that weather and time shorten (rain washes shoe marks, not a glove).",
'G14':"Doors, locks, keys, hiding places and set routes are one system in the map data: every door belongs to someone with a routine; every lock holds a find or a reason; every climbing place, drop-in, vault and car cut-through is marked with height, time and noise; a blocked way (a lorry across the dock road) makes people replan rather than stand.",
'G15':"The police case is one visible ladder for every crime, not only a constable's killing. It has the same pull-to-talk rule (LH-04) for anyone questioned, a front counter, custody with stances (silence included), identification by album or parade with its weight, bail and remand with dates, a finite number of officers drawn from their beats, observation posts and watches with a sign in the world, cases kept open while a knower lives, and pressure on a witness counted as a deed.",
'G16':"Officers are townspeople under one rule set: tempers, memory, off-duty routines, regard, and talk between them only through station talk, so two detectives told different stories compare them. A bought officer has a written limit for each kind of deed. Officers on a case may ask questions out of uniform through ordinary life (a cab ride, the caff), where what Frank says counts as a statement. Anyone who treats a wound (nurse, doctor) reads it and reports it by law dial and regard.",
'G17':"Holding a person is one state for anyone, a constable included: a held man walks where Frank walks, one night at most in a locked room (his ruling, nothing shown tied; a boot only if he says yes, decision 1), is missed by his routine, keeps every slot, and tells on release by law and grudge. Onlookers weigh the threat by temper; police never cede to it by rule. The held-man walk past watchers is his menace decision.",
'G18':"Fire is one system: it starts from a source at a spot, spreads by material and wind through rooms, terraces, cars and sheds at set rates, is seen and smelt by the perception rules, brings the brigade, can kill (his ruling), and leaves a seat the brigade reads as accidental or deliberate by fixed signs, with insurers and police following the reading.",
'G19':"One body and deed table serves every harm. It has the body rule for every weapon, a readable takedown that works when unseen and in reach and fails only on what the man could notice (his ruling, no hidden rolls), injuries that show their days, anyone can die but Frank, pulling a driver out as a confrontation, reckless driving and passengers as witnessed deeds, and damage to any object (a phone box, a lamp, a window) changing what it does until mended. The outcome of every fight (who won, who yielded, who ran) is part of the deed, so a beaten Frank loses menace with those who saw or heard.",
'G20':"The firm and every business run on their name, their week and their people: fare demand follows what the town says of Mickey's; fares are passengers with tempers who judge the cab; fare waves follow the town's timetable; the radio carries real work; drivers are the firm's eyes; the other firm is people who notice being undercut; a shop's week moves for a cause; any business or building can change hands; money is shown as 1990 objects.",
'G21':"Places beyond the map (the Tivoli cinema among them, in another district) stay off it for release and reach Frank by letters, phone calls and addresses: civic paperwork (if he rules it in), people who leave by the ferry or the north road and can be traced through whoever they told, places of record answering by what they hold, notes left and picked up by routine. Requests for a cinema, library or town hall are met by moving the find to a place that exists.",
'G22':"Story beats read what already happened, with every role a person carries passing to a named fallback two deep (being buyable included), so a principal killed early, a load taken early or Ellis gone leaves a way forward. Every beat and ending must be reachable by an honest or non-violent Frank through those fallbacks, never only by his own loud deed. The walk-out ending is a deliberate act, asked twice.",
'G23':"The street keeps real routines with real ends: every walker goes somewhere; each routine step shows its work (shutters, a swept step, bundles sorted); rain sends people to shelter they have a reason to use; props change by date; Frank's own routine is learned, so people with an ask come to his corner table at ten.",
'G24':"Assists change input or pace, never the rules: every verb on any single device, captions with who, where and how, every sound shown with its side, a reread and replay of talk, text settings for print, every motion effect on its own switch, warnings before permanent acts, and a save that holds every live state exactly. Nothing shows what others know or think.",
}
